QuantBot Attack Flow

Builds an “attack squad” up to maxAttackSquadSize (70) by scanning owned units that are active, healthy, in GUARD/AREAGUARD/AMBUSH, and not excluded types (harvester, MCV, carryall, saboteur, etc.).
First eligible unit becomes the leader; its closest enemy structure (or unit fallback) determines a shared destination.
For every squad member it:
Issues doMove2Pos toward that destination so everyone rallies on the same entry vector.
Immediately restores the unit’s previous guard point (to avoid post-combat drift).
Switches the unit to HUNT, clears any explicit attack position, and lets unit AI chase targets en route.
After the rally, QuantBot’s other routines (kiting/retreat, squad center tracking, carryall requests) keep nudging units toward the computed squad center, but the underlying hunt targeting is the same per-unit logic.
Classic AIPlayer Attack Flow

Much simpler loop: the first responding unit becomes leader, supplies a destination (closest enemy structure/unit).
Every eligible unit just gets doMove2Pos(destination) followed by doSetAttackMode(HUNT). It does not restore guard points or manage rally state further—once the move command is sent, the unit’s guard point stays at that destination.
No extra squad management, no size cap, no kiting logic; the unit AI handles everything after the initial move/hunt orders.
So QuantBot layers squad sizing, coordinated rallying, guard-point preservation, and follow-up behaviors on top of the same underlying per-unit hunt mechanics that AIPlayer uses.

